  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure is expected to begin to nose into the Upper OH \nValley during the day today. With an exiting low pressure \ncenter, expect a stronger gradient to persist. Winds are \nexpected to gust out of the west and southwest. Probs for\ngusting over 40 mph remains in the higher terrain at around 90%\nover Eastern Tucker and around 30% for the Laurel's. The lower\nterrain is a good 60% to 70% for gusts over 30 mph. \n\nBy the late Sunday to Monday morning timeframe, a disturbance \nwill cross the northern counties. Expect some precip here\nalthough amounts will be less.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe next weather feature will be a low pressure system arriving\nacross the Plains and into the Ohio Valley. Ahead of the front,\nthe probs are leaning more to a higher percentage between 20% \nand 40% for 0.50 and higher for 0.25\". The frontal passage will\noccur Thursday night with the potential for the first \naccumulating snow of the season. With northwest flow and the \nassist for the lake some probs show around 50% prob of an inch \nin the eastern Tucker and around 20% for the Laurel ridges. \n\nThus, expect an active pattern Thursday through at least \nSaturday that may include multiple periods of precipitation \nwhile likely pushing area temperature below normal. The latter \npoint also means that scenarios do exist for the first \naccumulating snow of the season, though mainly for northwest PA \nand the higher terrain.",
